00:01:18.790 --> 00:01:22.330
And it turns out that
84% of the companies

00:01:22.330 --> 00:01:26.290
were stuck in pilot
phase for over a year,

00:01:26.290 --> 00:01:29.440
and at 28% for over two years.

00:01:29.440 --> 00:01:34.416
And about 30% of
these pilots only

00:01:34.416 --> 00:01:35.540
could make it to the scale.

00:01:35.540 --> 00:01:37.480
So about 70% of
these pilots never

00:01:37.480 --> 00:01:39.410
made it to the production.

00:01:39.410 --> 00:01:41.950
So what's really going on?

00:01:41.950 --> 00:01:44.680
I mean, we call this
as a pilot purgatory.

00:01:44.680 --> 00:01:48.820
So essentially what's going
on is you started with pilots,

00:01:48.820 --> 00:01:53.030
and then you never
have success with it.

00:01:53.030 --> 00:01:56.450
So what are the key success
metrics which we found?

00:01:56.450 --> 00:02:00.050
It comes down to really
the business alignment.

00:02:00.050 --> 00:02:02.100
Are you aligned with
your senior executives?

00:02:02.100 --> 00:02:03.200
Do you have support?

00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:05.750
Is there a strong business
case or vision and how

00:02:05.750 --> 00:02:07.370
it will impact the business?

00:02:07.370 --> 00:02:10.259
Or is there internal
alignment in how

00:02:10.259 --> 00:02:15.240
do we execute, and a long
term perspective on that?

00:02:15.240 --> 00:02:17.510
So really, it comes
down to an ROI.

00:02:17.510 --> 00:02:20.270
If you can establish
ROI, you can easily

00:02:20.270 --> 00:02:21.230
get these alignments.

00:02:21.230 --> 00:02:24.990
And that's where you can move
from pilot to the next stage.

00:02:24.990 --> 00:02:29.240
So what we focus on here
is the why and not the how.

00:02:29.240 --> 00:02:31.960
Because many times, you start
off with the technology.

00:02:31.960 --> 00:02:35.630
You're proving out POC,
whether the technology works.

00:02:35.630 --> 00:02:37.640
And it starts off as a
very grassroots thing,

00:02:37.640 --> 00:02:41.120
but you want to really try
to go down to the making

00:02:41.120 --> 00:02:41.960
a business impact.

00:02:41.960 --> 00:02:44.930
And that's how you're
going to move from a pilot

00:02:44.930 --> 00:02:45.870
to the full scale.

00:02:49.030 --> 00:02:51.470
So when we think about
business outcomes

00:02:51.470 --> 00:02:54.110
and how you're going to
really start thinking about,

00:02:54.110 --> 00:02:56.330
it all starts with digitization.

00:02:56.330 --> 00:02:58.310
And what is really digitization?

00:02:58.310 --> 00:03:02.040
It's really a lot of things
which are analog today,

00:03:02.040 --> 00:03:04.460
they're your
physical blind spot.

00:03:04.460 --> 00:03:06.800
These are things which
are not connected.

00:03:06.800 --> 00:03:09.620
Or even if there is
data, they are in silos.

00:03:09.620 --> 00:03:11.120
So what you really
want to do is you

00:03:11.120 --> 00:03:13.130
want to connect these things--

00:03:13.130 --> 00:03:16.790
customers, staff, processes--
in a unified manner,

00:03:16.790 --> 00:03:18.660
in a unified system.

00:03:18.660 --> 00:03:21.050
So now you have all this
data coming together

00:03:21.050 --> 00:03:23.240
in a single platform.

00:03:23.240 --> 00:03:24.890
And from here on,
now you can start

00:03:24.890 --> 00:03:27.440
to analyze and start
creating these insights.

00:03:27.440 --> 00:03:31.940
And this is your
big data analytics.

00:03:31.940 --> 00:03:35.430
On your past data,
historical data,

00:03:35.430 --> 00:03:38.560
you can start to see what
things were going on,

00:03:38.560 --> 00:03:41.900
what was the process, how things
were happening in your business

00:03:41.900 --> 00:03:43.380
environment.

00:03:43.380 --> 00:03:45.980
But then you want to
also start to look at,

00:03:45.980 --> 00:03:47.990
can you create
prediction models?

00:03:47.990 --> 00:03:51.530
Can you use machine learning
and advanced artificial

00:03:51.530 --> 00:03:54.930
intelligence to create
predictive models

00:03:54.930 --> 00:03:59.390
for your business,
which is really

00:03:59.390 --> 00:04:02.739
indicative of the key
performance indicators?

00:04:02.739 --> 00:04:05.030
And then you want to use all
of this information, which

00:04:05.030 --> 00:04:06.500
is the real time
information coming

00:04:06.500 --> 00:04:10.250
from the business, the
past behavior, past data

00:04:10.250 --> 00:04:12.680
and insights, the
predictive model, the what's

00:04:12.680 --> 00:04:15.680
going to happen in the
future, to really start

00:04:15.680 --> 00:04:16.890
to think about workflows.

00:04:16.890 --> 00:04:19.139
And that's very important,
because there are workflows

00:04:19.139 --> 00:04:19.806
happening today.

00:04:19.806 --> 00:04:20.930
But they are not connected.

00:04:20.930 --> 00:04:21.880
They are in isolation.

00:04:21.880 --> 00:04:23.296
You want to think
about, how can I

00:04:23.296 --> 00:04:28.340
take that process, which is in
physical form happening today,

00:04:28.340 --> 00:04:31.730
can we make it more digitized?

00:04:31.730 --> 00:04:35.240
Can we make it all
connected in a seamless way?

00:04:35.240 --> 00:04:39.140
And that's where you can start
to start optimizing and get

00:04:39.140 --> 00:04:41.790
benefit from IoT.

00:04:44.810 --> 00:04:47.510
So when we look at
IoT ROI, so it really

00:04:47.510 --> 00:04:50.360
comes down to that you
want to be measuring

00:04:50.360 --> 00:04:52.330
ROI, Return on Investment.

00:04:52.330 --> 00:04:55.220
So there is a direct
ROI, which is essentially

00:04:55.220 --> 00:04:56.570
revenues and costs.

00:04:56.570 --> 00:04:58.520
So you want to
increase your revenues.

00:04:58.520 --> 00:05:00.170
Can you sell more?

00:05:00.170 --> 00:05:01.972
Or can you reduce your cost?

00:05:01.972 --> 00:05:03.680
Are there things you
can do to reduce it?

00:05:03.680 --> 00:05:05.180
So that's direct.

00:05:05.180 --> 00:05:06.920
And there are things
you can do using IoT

00:05:06.920 --> 00:05:11.060
because, with IoT, you have
more data, more insights.

00:05:11.060 --> 00:05:14.690
So you can enable
features, enable sales,

00:05:14.690 --> 00:05:15.890
service enablement.

00:05:15.890 --> 00:05:21.560
You can do improved pricing,
measured demand and elasticity.

00:05:21.560 --> 00:05:24.110
You can also look at
the costs and can we

00:05:24.110 --> 00:05:28.490
improve efficiencies,
uptime, reduce the risk,

00:05:28.490 --> 00:05:30.620
and improve quality?

00:05:30.620 --> 00:05:35.300
But then there are
other benefits of IoT

00:05:35.300 --> 00:05:37.860
which you can expand to.

00:05:37.860 --> 00:05:39.500
For example, marketing.

00:05:39.500 --> 00:05:43.590
And this is where you want to
know your end users better.

00:05:43.590 --> 00:05:47.330
Can you use the data to
understand their behavior?

00:05:47.330 --> 00:05:50.400
If they are connected through
IoT, you actually can.

00:05:50.400 --> 00:05:53.880
And you can then upsell
and cross-sell products.

00:05:53.880 --> 00:05:57.010
And you can do personalization
based on that customer.

00:05:57.010 --> 00:05:59.160
You can also build better
products because now you

00:05:59.160 --> 00:06:00.810
understand the customers.

00:06:00.810 --> 00:06:02.900
You understand their behaviors.

00:06:02.900 --> 00:06:04.860
And in general, you have
a better relationship

00:06:04.860 --> 00:06:06.176
with the customer.

00:06:06.176 --> 00:06:07.300
They connect with you more.

00:06:07.300 --> 00:06:09.810
You have a better brand.

00:06:09.810 --> 00:06:12.820
On the engagement side-- so when
it comes to service delivery

00:06:12.820 --> 00:06:16.710
especially, you want to have
that deep customer knowledge

00:06:16.710 --> 00:06:18.480
of how they use their product.

00:06:18.480 --> 00:06:20.490
Can we proactively
reach out to them?

00:06:20.490 --> 00:06:22.140
Can we help them?

00:06:22.140 --> 00:06:25.614
Can we make their
experience very delightful?

00:06:25.614 --> 00:06:27.780
And also, with all the data
which is coming through,

00:06:27.780 --> 00:06:30.420
you actually start to understand
a lot of opportunities

00:06:30.420 --> 00:06:31.800
which were in your blind spot.

00:06:31.800 --> 00:06:35.700
And you start to see how
can you use that data

00:06:35.700 --> 00:06:39.600
to mine new businesses and
create new business outcomes

00:06:39.600 --> 00:06:41.390
which were not possible before.

00:06:44.790 --> 00:06:49.470
So let's go into a few examples
of our different verticals.

00:06:49.470 --> 00:06:51.270
Hopefully, this will
resonate with you

00:06:51.270 --> 00:06:53.010
and give you an
idea of how you want

00:06:53.010 --> 00:06:57.530
to start to think about
different use cases in IoT.

00:06:57.530 --> 00:06:58.780
So for example, manufacturing.

00:06:58.780 --> 00:06:59.920
So if you think
about manufacturing,

00:06:59.920 --> 00:07:00.920
what's really important?

00:07:00.920 --> 00:07:06.660
I mean, the important thing in
manufacturing is productivity.

00:07:06.660 --> 00:07:09.270
Factories are
operational 24 by 7.

00:07:09.270 --> 00:07:10.710
Can we keep the uptime?

00:07:10.710 --> 00:07:12.840
Can it be more efficient?

00:07:12.840 --> 00:07:13.950
Can it be more safe?

00:07:13.950 --> 00:07:15.750
Because many of
these manufacturing

00:07:15.750 --> 00:07:21.640
heavy industries inherently
have challenges there,

00:07:21.640 --> 00:07:25.110
because it's a live environment
and a very high temperature

00:07:25.110 --> 00:07:27.790
and extreme environment there.

00:07:27.790 --> 00:07:31.770
So you could use
predictive maintenance.

00:07:31.770 --> 00:07:33.570
We'll talk about some
of that later on.

00:07:33.570 --> 00:07:36.540
But essentially use artificial
intelligence and machine

00:07:36.540 --> 00:07:39.547
learning to create predictive
models for your machines.

00:07:39.547 --> 00:07:41.130
So now you have a
better understanding

00:07:41.130 --> 00:07:44.520
of when these machines is going
to fail so you can take action

00:07:44.520 --> 00:07:45.040
up front.

00:07:45.040 --> 00:07:47.430
You can also understand
what are the factors which

00:07:47.430 --> 00:07:50.970
are driving the failures
so you can work on that

00:07:50.970 --> 00:07:53.750
and extend the life
of your machine.

00:07:53.750 --> 00:07:57.000
So essentially, you're not
only improving the asset

00:07:57.000 --> 00:08:01.080
utilization, but you're
reducing the downtime.

00:08:01.080 --> 00:08:05.630
The second aspect of
manufacturing is the process.

00:08:05.630 --> 00:08:08.890
There could be inventory supply
chain or different processes.

00:08:08.890 --> 00:08:11.040
For example, in a car,
it goes through paint.

00:08:11.040 --> 00:08:14.310
It goes through a
retrofitting, testing.

00:08:14.310 --> 00:08:16.200
And you want to understand
how much time it's

00:08:16.200 --> 00:08:17.366
taking between each process.

00:08:17.366 --> 00:08:19.060
You want to optimize for that.

00:08:19.060 --> 00:08:21.690
And if there is an inventory
which has excess piling up,

00:08:21.690 --> 00:08:23.350
you want to reduce that.

00:08:23.350 --> 00:08:24.870
So these are all
different processes

00:08:24.870 --> 00:08:29.210
you can understand by using
IoT and getting real time

00:08:29.210 --> 00:08:31.440
insight of what's going on.

00:08:31.440 --> 00:08:33.510
And then not only
in the factory,

00:08:33.510 --> 00:08:36.360
but also especially in
just-in-time manufacturing,

00:08:36.360 --> 00:08:37.740
it goes beyond the factory.

00:08:37.740 --> 00:08:39.299
It's also connected
to your vendors

00:08:39.299 --> 00:08:42.150
and upstream and
downstream supply chains.

00:08:42.150 --> 00:08:47.010
And you want to get a very
good sense of how the supply

00:08:47.010 --> 00:08:48.270
chain is functioning.

00:08:48.270 --> 00:08:51.330
Is there going to be any
shortages of components

00:08:51.330 --> 00:08:54.510
that may impact your
production and also, maybe,

00:08:54.510 --> 00:08:59.220
customer experience in terms
of delivery of these products?

00:08:59.220 --> 00:09:01.890
And in general, you
want to understand

00:09:01.890 --> 00:09:04.980
how your employees are doing,
how your machines are doing,

00:09:04.980 --> 00:09:06.600
and can you improve
your productivity

00:09:06.600 --> 00:09:09.510
and reduce your
failure rates and have

00:09:09.510 --> 00:09:13.330
a high ROI for your customers?

00:09:13.330 --> 00:09:17.500
There are many partners, like
SpringML, Relayr, Tellmeplus,

00:09:17.500 --> 00:09:18.340
Clear Object.

00:09:18.340 --> 00:09:19.756
These are different
partners which

00:09:19.756 --> 00:09:24.600
are actually using Google
Cloud Platform to deliver that.

00:09:24.600 --> 00:09:27.300
And clearly, if you have
use cases around that,

00:09:27.300 --> 00:09:29.310
you can also reach out to us.

00:09:29.310 --> 00:09:33.600
And we can help on that too.

00:09:33.600 --> 00:09:36.220
Likewise, on oil and gas,
it's slightly different.

00:09:36.220 --> 00:09:39.660
The same extreme use cases,
but here the business outcomes

00:09:39.660 --> 00:09:42.300
are really around,
first of all, can I

00:09:42.300 --> 00:09:45.930
get better resource mapping?

00:09:45.930 --> 00:09:49.290
Can I understand where the
next set of oil is going to be?

00:09:49.290 --> 00:09:51.330
Can I use machine
learning, and can I

00:09:51.330 --> 00:09:56.340
use satellite image data
to get that understanding?

00:09:56.340 --> 00:10:00.030
Also I want to
optimize the machines,

00:10:00.030 --> 00:10:03.730
and their failure is important,
and where different assets are,

00:10:03.730 --> 00:10:05.700
the trucks are,
the real machines.

00:10:05.700 --> 00:10:07.480
And even employees
are your assets.

00:10:07.480 --> 00:10:10.299
And this is where you want
to understand, are they safe?

00:10:10.299 --> 00:10:11.590
Are they in a safe environment?

00:10:11.590 --> 00:10:13.006
If there is going
to be a problem,

00:10:13.006 --> 00:10:16.240
you want to predict that.

00:10:16.240 --> 00:10:18.480
And if there is a problem,
you want to evacuate.

00:10:18.480 --> 00:10:20.400
And the safety is
very important.

00:10:20.400 --> 00:10:24.440
So these are the key use
cases in oil and gas.

00:10:24.440 --> 00:10:30.690
There are partners like Cisco,
Losant, again, Relayr, FogHorn.

00:10:30.690 --> 00:10:36.130
They operate within the
whole IoT architecture.

00:10:36.130 --> 00:10:39.760
Some are more in Edge,
some are in Cloud.

00:10:39.760 --> 00:10:43.770
But there are a lot of
partners Google has which

00:10:43.770 --> 00:10:45.510
can help in these use cases.

00:10:48.530 --> 00:10:49.930
Let's talk about logistics.

00:10:49.930 --> 00:10:52.210
Primarily, when we say
smart transportation,

00:10:52.210 --> 00:10:54.410
it's really about logistics.

00:10:54.410 --> 00:10:57.780
And that could be a vertical
by itself, but it also feeds--

00:10:57.780 --> 00:10:59.030
we talked about manufacturing.

00:10:59.030 --> 00:11:03.220
And even in retail, it feeds
the rest of the industries,

00:11:03.220 --> 00:11:04.090
as well.

00:11:04.090 --> 00:11:07.090
And here, the asset tracking,
clearly-- like these trucks

00:11:07.090 --> 00:11:07.899
need to be tracked.

00:11:07.899 --> 00:11:09.190
So asset tracking is important.

00:11:09.190 --> 00:11:11.350
But many times, there
is also the product

00:11:11.350 --> 00:11:13.830
on those trucks,
which is important.

00:11:13.830 --> 00:11:15.610
And you want to
understand, for example,

00:11:15.610 --> 00:11:17.260
if it's a cold storage,
you want to understand how

00:11:17.260 --> 00:11:18.860
the temperature conditions are.

00:11:18.860 --> 00:11:20.920
If you're transporting
something which

00:11:20.920 --> 00:11:24.000
needs to be maintained
at a certain temperature,

00:11:24.000 --> 00:11:27.160
then the telematics
of these vehicles,

00:11:27.160 --> 00:11:30.400
how fast they're going, are
they following the route,

00:11:30.400 --> 00:11:32.830
did they deviate
from their route,

00:11:32.830 --> 00:11:34.720
needs to be notified
in real time

00:11:34.720 --> 00:11:36.400
so you can take action on that.

00:11:36.400 --> 00:11:38.290
And then also, just
like any asset--

00:11:38.290 --> 00:11:40.080
we talked about
predictive maintenance--

00:11:40.080 --> 00:11:44.350
you want to understand
the predictions of when

00:11:44.350 --> 00:11:48.370
these assets are going to fail.

00:11:48.370 --> 00:11:51.530
Can we do something to
enhance their useful life?

00:11:51.530 --> 00:11:55.075
What are the key failure
modes for these assets?

00:11:57.830 --> 00:12:01.040
So let's talk about health care.

00:12:01.040 --> 00:12:04.020
So we'll talk about health
care, and pharma, and medtech.

00:12:04.020 --> 00:12:08.070
They're two slightly different,
but very similar use cases.

00:12:08.070 --> 00:12:10.610
So health care, this is
about, for example, hospitals.

00:12:10.610 --> 00:12:15.040
This is hospitals or
extended day cares where

00:12:15.040 --> 00:12:16.300
you're dealing with patients.

00:12:16.300 --> 00:12:19.870
And really, what the main goal
for a lot of these hospitals

00:12:19.870 --> 00:12:24.230
and in the health care industry
is really to serve patients.

00:12:24.230 --> 00:12:27.025
And the patient experience
is the most important thing.

00:12:27.025 --> 00:12:29.240
Their health is the
most important thing.

00:12:29.240 --> 00:12:34.386
So you're using IoT to, first
of all, A, measure the assets.

00:12:34.386 --> 00:12:36.010
So there are lots of
assets, like where

00:12:36.010 --> 00:12:40.650
the wheelchairs are, where the
beds are, where the IV pump is.

00:12:40.650 --> 00:12:44.380
So when there is a
need for it, the nurses

00:12:44.380 --> 00:12:45.610
don't have to go look for it.

00:12:45.610 --> 00:12:48.590
And they can address the
patient as fast as possible.

00:12:48.590 --> 00:12:51.280
Also what happens
is, because there's

00:12:51.280 --> 00:12:54.040
so much unpredictability
around these assets,

00:12:54.040 --> 00:12:56.470
you end up buying
more than you need.

00:12:56.470 --> 00:12:57.814
And it increases your costs.

00:12:57.814 --> 00:12:59.230
And these are
expensive equipment.

00:12:59.230 --> 00:13:02.350
And you end up having a
lot more of inventory.

00:13:02.350 --> 00:13:05.900
And that adds to
your cost, overall.

00:13:05.900 --> 00:13:08.620
And then we know about
this connected health,

00:13:08.620 --> 00:13:13.210
use cases around monitoring
of key vitals of the patients

00:13:13.210 --> 00:13:19.360
while they are at the facility,
like mental health patients,

00:13:19.360 --> 00:13:22.900
or infants, et
cetera, where you want

00:13:22.900 --> 00:13:26.010
to make sure either they
don't leave the facility,

00:13:26.010 --> 00:13:27.610
and the key vitals are there.

00:13:27.610 --> 00:13:30.760
If they fall, you want to be
able to detect fall detection

00:13:30.760 --> 00:13:32.980
and take appropriate action
as quickly as possible.

00:13:36.140 --> 00:13:39.320
Then let's go into retail.

00:13:39.320 --> 00:13:42.410
There are lots of
use cases in retail.

00:13:42.410 --> 00:13:45.470
We're already familiar
with many of them.

00:13:45.470 --> 00:13:48.110
And we can all
associate with it.

00:13:48.110 --> 00:13:50.900
So one of the key ones,
especially for the business,

00:13:50.900 --> 00:13:53.810
comes down to better
engagement with the customer.

00:13:53.810 --> 00:13:57.770
Because for retail, the
experience in the store

00:13:57.770 --> 00:13:59.105
is the most important thing.

00:13:59.105 --> 00:14:00.480
And can you use
the technologies,

00:14:00.480 --> 00:14:02.900
like beacons, cameras,
and geolocation

00:14:02.900 --> 00:14:08.870
based notifications to
create these experiences,

00:14:08.870 --> 00:14:13.490
and location based notifications
and marketing on-site.

00:14:13.490 --> 00:14:16.480
You may want to also use
image detection and video

00:14:16.480 --> 00:14:18.920
analysis to look at footfall.

00:14:18.920 --> 00:14:22.070
Or you can use RFIDs
for that and understand

00:14:22.070 --> 00:14:26.300
what parts of the store
are high traffic areas.

00:14:26.300 --> 00:14:29.450
And can they be used to improve
your promotions and product

00:14:29.450 --> 00:14:35.060
placement, and in general the
way you operate your business?

00:14:35.060 --> 00:14:37.850
Automated checkouts are
also very important,

00:14:37.850 --> 00:14:41.840
because it's part of
the customer experience.

00:14:41.840 --> 00:14:45.430
And can you do more with
IoT with understanding

00:14:45.430 --> 00:14:47.810
who the customer is,
what products they have,

00:14:47.810 --> 00:14:50.030
what account is associated
with to really improve

00:14:50.030 --> 00:14:52.490
that analysis?

00:14:52.490 --> 00:14:54.216
And then there are
a lot of use cases,

00:14:54.216 --> 00:14:56.090
not just around customer
experience, but also

00:14:56.090 --> 00:14:57.590
the back store operations.

00:14:57.590 --> 00:15:00.200
And a lot of that is about
the best customer experience,

00:15:00.200 --> 00:15:02.110
but also about inventory.

00:15:02.110 --> 00:15:05.090
Do you have the right
amount of inventory at hand?

00:15:05.090 --> 00:15:09.070
Is there a lack of inventory?

00:15:09.070 --> 00:15:11.600
Can you replenish
quickly so, when

00:15:11.600 --> 00:15:14.900
customers come looking for
something, it doesn't run out?

00:15:14.900 --> 00:15:18.260
And then can you also have
a better understanding

00:15:18.260 --> 00:15:23.030
of how the products are
moving on your shelf?

00:15:23.030 --> 00:15:24.920
What are the fast
mover products?

00:15:24.920 --> 00:15:26.220
What are slow movers?

00:15:26.220 --> 00:15:30.830
Can you optimize a
placement on the shelf?

00:15:30.830 --> 00:15:33.350
Let's talk about
pharma and medtech.

00:15:33.350 --> 00:15:38.120
So this is about
pharmaceutical companies

00:15:38.120 --> 00:15:40.490
and medical technology
companies who

00:15:40.490 --> 00:15:44.180
are in the business
of helping customers

00:15:44.180 --> 00:15:45.860
from a medication standpoint.

00:15:45.860 --> 00:15:50.210
And their main goal is to
ensure that the customers

00:15:50.210 --> 00:15:53.630
or the patients are taking
medicines appropriately.

00:15:53.630 --> 00:15:55.710
And it's not only good
for the customers,

00:15:55.710 --> 00:15:59.180
but also good for the business
where they can improve

00:15:59.180 --> 00:16:01.890
the adherence of the medicines.

00:16:01.890 --> 00:16:05.040
That way, they can
improve their revenues.

00:16:05.040 --> 00:16:08.480
But also having a better
patient engagement

00:16:08.480 --> 00:16:10.970
not only improves their
brand, but it also

00:16:10.970 --> 00:16:13.596
allows them to do
automated interventions.

00:16:13.596 --> 00:16:16.520
So if a patient is
not taking medicines,

00:16:16.520 --> 00:16:18.710
they can notify them.

00:16:18.710 --> 00:16:24.930
Or if it's a connected
insulin meter where

00:16:24.930 --> 00:16:27.050
you could measure
the insulin levels

00:16:27.050 --> 00:16:30.590
and inject appropriate amount
of insulin to the patient,

00:16:30.590 --> 00:16:33.604
that can be done
automatedly as well.

00:16:33.604 --> 00:16:35.270
And then there's a
big part about pharma

00:16:35.270 --> 00:16:38.330
and medical is that they spend a
lot of time in the FDA process,

00:16:38.330 --> 00:16:39.987
in the regulatory process.

00:16:39.987 --> 00:16:42.320
And they have to go through
these clinical trials, which

00:16:42.320 --> 00:16:43.310
is very expensive.

00:16:43.310 --> 00:16:45.500
And oftentimes, a lot
of these clinical trials

00:16:45.500 --> 00:16:48.290
fail because they just don't
have enough sample points.

00:16:48.290 --> 00:16:51.410
But with IoT, you can actually
improve and accelerate that.

00:16:51.410 --> 00:16:54.680
Because now, you can start to
get a lot of the data live.

00:16:54.680 --> 00:16:57.320
And that will improve
the way you go to market.

00:16:57.320 --> 00:16:59.890
And you can launch
your products faster.

00:17:02.440 --> 00:17:02.940
OK.

00:17:02.940 --> 00:17:06.990
So let's talk about
Google Cloud IoT.

00:17:06.990 --> 00:17:08.680
What do we mean by that?

00:17:08.680 --> 00:17:13.452
So when we look at the
Google Cloud IoT Platform,

00:17:13.452 --> 00:17:14.910
you want to think
from both devices

00:17:14.910 --> 00:17:15.951
all the way to the Cloud.

00:17:15.951 --> 00:17:18.930
So on the very extreme
is the devices.

00:17:18.930 --> 00:17:23.280
And these are the devices,
which could be Edge device,

00:17:23.280 --> 00:17:23.829
for example.

00:17:23.829 --> 00:17:25.370
And we have a product
that we'll talk

00:17:25.370 --> 00:17:27.960
about shortly is the
Cloud IoT Edge, which

00:17:27.960 --> 00:17:29.230
allows you to do three things.

00:17:29.230 --> 00:17:32.840
One is it allows you to
connect to the Google Cloud.

00:17:32.840 --> 00:17:36.720
This is a product called
Edge IoT core, which does it

00:17:36.720 --> 00:17:39.600
securely, and then makes
sure the connection is

00:17:39.600 --> 00:17:45.960
strong and easy for you
to develop as a product.

00:17:45.960 --> 00:17:50.490
And second is the Edge ML, which
is how can you do inference

00:17:50.490 --> 00:17:54.300
on the device and machine
learning on the device

00:17:54.300 --> 00:17:59.500
and create those outcomes
without relying on the cloud?

00:17:59.500 --> 00:18:00.780
We do support Android Things.

00:18:00.780 --> 00:18:05.100
This is a Google OS
for IoT devices, which

00:18:05.100 --> 00:18:08.350
is hardened and
built specifically

00:18:08.350 --> 00:18:11.260
for IoT for the best security.

00:18:11.260 --> 00:18:13.800
But we also support
other operating systems,

00:18:13.800 --> 00:18:17.100
like embedded OSes
and Linux OS as well.

00:18:17.100 --> 00:18:22.540
And finally, these are the
CPUs or the processor units.

00:18:22.540 --> 00:18:25.560
So there's CPU, or the
general purpose or MCUs

00:18:25.560 --> 00:18:31.830
can be GPUs for advanced
machine learning capabilities.

00:18:31.830 --> 00:18:34.710
And then we have a dedicated
product we just recently

00:18:34.710 --> 00:18:38.610
launched called Edge TPU,
which is for machine learning

00:18:38.610 --> 00:18:41.040
inferences on the chip itself.

00:18:41.040 --> 00:18:43.110
And it's a hardware accelerator.

00:18:43.110 --> 00:18:45.060
We'll talk about
it shortly, too.

00:18:45.060 --> 00:18:48.870
And on the right side
is the Cloud IoT Core,

00:18:48.870 --> 00:18:51.060
which is the entrance
point for all the IoT

00:18:51.060 --> 00:18:52.810
data into the cloud.

00:18:52.810 --> 00:18:54.900
And this is basically
your gateway

00:18:54.900 --> 00:18:58.140
to inside the cloud for
the rest of the products.

00:18:58.140 --> 00:19:03.000
It basically pumps all this data
into Cloud Pub/Sub, which is

00:19:03.000 --> 00:19:05.190
essentially your message queue.

00:19:05.190 --> 00:19:10.620
And from thereon, different
products within Cloud IoT

00:19:10.620 --> 00:19:12.820
can actually take up this data.

00:19:12.820 --> 00:19:15.660
For example, you can use
Cloud Functions to create

00:19:15.660 --> 00:19:17.380
your own applications.

00:19:17.380 --> 00:19:21.660
You can also use Cloud Data
Flow to do ETL operations

00:19:21.660 --> 00:19:24.870
and transform this data
and store it, for example,

00:19:24.870 --> 00:19:27.750
in BigQuery or BigTable.

00:19:27.750 --> 00:19:31.320
And then use machine
learning to get this data

00:19:31.320 --> 00:19:36.960
from these databases, create
machine learning models,

00:19:36.960 --> 00:19:39.360
and then do either
inference on the cloud,

00:19:39.360 --> 00:19:43.740
or you can send these
models back to the Edge ML

00:19:43.740 --> 00:19:48.050
to create these inferences
at the Edge, as well.

00:19:48.050 --> 00:19:50.460
And then you obviously have
the visualization layer

00:19:50.460 --> 00:19:51.144
on top of that.

00:19:51.144 --> 00:19:52.060
That's the last thing.

00:19:52.060 --> 00:19:54.690
You could use Cloud
Data Lab, Data Studio,

00:19:54.690 --> 00:19:58.970
or maybe build your own insights
on top of the Cloud platform

00:19:58.970 --> 00:20:00.470
to create those
visualization charts

00:20:00.470 --> 00:20:02.760
and reporting, et cetera.

00:20:02.760 --> 00:20:05.080
So that sort of, hopefully,
gives you an idea.

00:20:05.080 --> 00:20:07.140
We also have a
Google Maps platform,

00:20:07.140 --> 00:20:10.470
which is also a Google
product, which allows you

00:20:10.470 --> 00:20:12.930
to get better location updates.

00:20:12.930 --> 00:20:14.970
And you can feed
that information back

00:20:14.970 --> 00:20:17.970
into the Google Cloud
Platform to create,

00:20:17.970 --> 00:20:23.668
for example, asset tracking
use cases on top of that.

00:20:23.668 --> 00:20:28.362
So let's talk about
why Google Cloud IoT,

00:20:28.362 --> 00:20:30.570
and what's so
differentiated about it?

00:20:30.570 --> 00:20:32.150
So first of all,
Android Things, we

00:20:32.150 --> 00:20:36.260
have a unified end-to-end
integration where seamlessly it

00:20:36.260 --> 00:20:38.237
connects with Android
Things and other OS

00:20:38.237 --> 00:20:39.320
and connects to the cloud.

00:20:39.320 --> 00:20:41.111
So it makes the
development of the hardware

00:20:41.111 --> 00:20:43.190
and IoT devices easy.

00:20:43.190 --> 00:20:44.900
It's a global IoT service.

00:20:44.900 --> 00:20:50.540
It's purely a serverless
service where you don't have

00:20:50.540 --> 00:20:53.130
to maintain different hubs.

00:20:53.130 --> 00:20:54.890
You simply spin up your devices.

00:20:54.890 --> 00:20:57.690
You only care about device's
data coming through.

00:20:57.690 --> 00:21:00.350
You don't have to
worry about scale.

00:21:00.350 --> 00:21:01.790
And it has
intelligence built-in.

00:21:01.790 --> 00:21:06.250
So it applies machine
learning and AI in the cloud.

00:21:06.250 --> 00:21:10.979
It allows you to create IoT
which is not simply basic use

00:21:10.979 --> 00:21:12.770
cases, for example,
temperature monitoring,

00:21:12.770 --> 00:21:17.750
but also create prediction
models around temperature.

00:21:17.750 --> 00:21:20.340
As I mentioned, it's
serverless by design.

00:21:20.340 --> 00:21:22.400
And this is true, just
like BigQuery and many

00:21:22.400 --> 00:21:24.120
of these products.

00:21:24.120 --> 00:21:27.771
So it basically is a
global network backbone

00:21:27.771 --> 00:21:29.270
and a front end
which is essentially

00:21:29.270 --> 00:21:33.980
powering the billion-plus
user applications from Google.

00:21:33.980 --> 00:21:37.220
And pretty much all
Google applications

00:21:37.220 --> 00:21:40.400
are built on the
Google Cloud Platform.

00:21:40.400 --> 00:21:44.140
And then using the
same network backbone--

00:21:44.140 --> 00:21:48.030
so this is what is powering
the Google Cloud IoT.

00:21:48.030 --> 00:21:51.680
And you can take the
advantage of that network

00:21:51.680 --> 00:21:54.110
for your application and
can build applications

00:21:54.110 --> 00:21:58.480
with the same level of
reliability and scale.

00:21:58.480 --> 00:22:01.430
So let's talk about
Cloud IoT Edge.

00:22:01.430 --> 00:22:05.390
So if you step back, there
is the Edge computing,

00:22:05.390 --> 00:22:08.780
which is along back
when there was no Cloud.

00:22:08.780 --> 00:22:12.170
It was all on-prem.

00:22:12.170 --> 00:22:15.260
But there has been a lot
of advantages to the cloud.

00:22:15.260 --> 00:22:18.170
And people have been really
migrating to the Cloud.

00:22:18.170 --> 00:22:20.300
But there are some
use cases where

00:22:20.300 --> 00:22:23.880
there is a little bit of
Edge which is still there.

00:22:23.880 --> 00:22:26.840
And that's important
from various use cases.

00:22:26.840 --> 00:22:29.950
For example, it
could be security.

00:22:29.950 --> 00:22:33.830
It could be the fact is that
you don't want some data.

00:22:33.830 --> 00:22:35.570
It's mostly part
of policy that you

00:22:35.570 --> 00:22:38.690
don't want some part of your
data to leave on-premises

00:22:38.690 --> 00:22:39.290
to the cloud.

00:22:39.290 --> 00:22:43.650
So can you do
processing on the Edge?

00:22:43.650 --> 00:22:45.890
There could be use cases,
like robotics, where

00:22:45.890 --> 00:22:47.930
the latency is very important.

00:22:47.930 --> 00:22:51.530
So you don't want the round
trip delay times to the cloud.

00:22:51.530 --> 00:22:55.120
So you may want to take
certain actions quickly,

00:22:55.120 --> 00:22:57.380
and hence, you want
to do Edge computing.

00:22:57.380 --> 00:22:59.531
Likewise, for
predictive maintenance,

00:22:59.531 --> 00:23:02.030
we talked about a use case about
machine learning inference.

00:23:02.030 --> 00:23:06.620
You may actually be doing
Cloud learning, ML learning

00:23:06.620 --> 00:23:10.160
in the cloud, but you maybe
want to do the inference

00:23:10.160 --> 00:23:13.530
in the device itself.

00:23:13.530 --> 00:23:16.940
Likewise, object recognition--
so here if you want to be

00:23:16.940 --> 00:23:18.586
taking quick--

00:23:18.586 --> 00:23:20.210
for example, it's a
manufacturing line,

00:23:20.210 --> 00:23:24.640
and you want to be
testing for defects.

00:23:24.640 --> 00:23:27.629
And in order for you to
have the maximum yield

00:23:27.629 --> 00:23:29.170
and the high
throughput, you're going

00:23:29.170 --> 00:23:31.490
to be doing the fast
object recognition.

00:23:31.490 --> 00:23:36.920
And again, it's an inference
on-prem on the device itself,

00:23:36.920 --> 00:23:39.802
and that's something
you want to do quickly.

00:23:39.802 --> 00:23:42.260
Likewise, there are use cases--
if you're all familiar with

00:23:42.260 --> 00:23:43.810
smart vehicles--

00:23:43.810 --> 00:23:46.840
warehousing where
there are AGVs.

00:23:46.840 --> 00:23:50.270
And here, also, you want to
be taking actions quickly.

00:23:50.270 --> 00:23:53.540
Or maybe the WAN connectivity
is not that reliable,

00:23:53.540 --> 00:23:58.440
and hence, you want
to do Edge computing.

00:23:58.440 --> 00:24:02.405
So what is Cloud IoT Edge?

00:24:02.405 --> 00:24:05.030
So as we talked about,
it extends the cloud data

00:24:05.030 --> 00:24:06.680
processing down to the Edge.

00:24:06.680 --> 00:24:10.490
It runs on Android Things
and other operating systems.

00:24:10.490 --> 00:24:16.715
It connects to the cloud
using the Edge IoT core.

00:24:16.715 --> 00:24:20.930
It stores, processes,
filters, derives intelligence.

00:24:20.930 --> 00:24:25.070
And then you can run inferences
for the TensorFlow Lite models,

00:24:25.070 --> 00:24:26.360
locally.

00:24:26.360 --> 00:24:28.790
And it allows you to
do next-gen of machine

00:24:28.790 --> 00:24:30.320
learning at the Edge.

00:24:32.860 --> 00:24:35.010
And this is how it really works.

00:24:35.010 --> 00:24:38.190
You would start with the
building and the training

00:24:38.190 --> 00:24:40.140
of the ML model in the cloud.

00:24:40.140 --> 00:24:43.470
You aggregate the data
coming from the devices.

00:24:43.470 --> 00:24:44.610
You aggregate the data.

00:24:44.610 --> 00:24:46.980
You use a machine learning
model to train it,

00:24:46.980 --> 00:24:49.936
and then you send it back.

00:24:49.936 --> 00:24:51.310
Essentially, what
you would do is

00:24:51.310 --> 00:24:54.000
you would convert that machine
learning model from TensorFlow

00:24:54.000 --> 00:24:55.680
to TensorFlow Lite.

00:24:55.680 --> 00:24:59.650
And then you would compile
it for the given target.

00:24:59.650 --> 00:25:05.040
If it is the GPU, or
general purpose CPU,

00:25:05.040 --> 00:25:08.870
or if it is the Edge TPU,
which I will talk next,

00:25:08.870 --> 00:25:12.679
you would want to compile
it for that platform.

00:25:12.679 --> 00:25:14.970
And then you would install
it, essentially send it back

00:25:14.970 --> 00:25:16.990
to the device.

00:25:16.990 --> 00:25:21.760
And you will start to do
inference on the device itself.

00:25:21.760 --> 00:25:23.620
So we talked about
Edge TPU several times.

00:25:23.620 --> 00:25:25.160
So what really is it?

00:25:25.160 --> 00:25:29.652
So it is a very tiny, high
performance, inference chip

00:25:29.652 --> 00:25:34.090
set, which is not a
general purpose CPU.

00:25:34.090 --> 00:25:38.020
It is custom built
for machine learning.

00:25:38.020 --> 00:25:39.580
It can do fast iterations.

00:25:39.580 --> 00:25:44.430
And it's the first phase is
to build for video and vision,

00:25:44.430 --> 00:25:47.640
because these are very
high CPU-intensive.

00:25:47.640 --> 00:25:52.180
And it can run concurrently
at 30-plus frames per second.

00:25:52.180 --> 00:25:55.950
This allows you to create high
performance machine learning

00:25:55.950 --> 00:26:03.060
at the edge, with the low power
consumption, lower footprint.

00:26:03.060 --> 00:26:09.750
And also, because it works
seamlessly with the cloud,

00:26:09.750 --> 00:26:14.750
it's an end-to-end
implementation, not isolated

00:26:14.750 --> 00:26:17.190
where you have to put it
all together yourself.

00:26:17.190 --> 00:26:22.170
So you basically use
Cloud IoT core and Google

00:26:22.170 --> 00:26:25.900
Cloud for machine learning,
and send these machine

00:26:25.900 --> 00:26:28.427
learning models back
to the Edge TPU.

00:26:28.427 --> 00:26:29.510
And you can do inferences.

00:26:29.510 --> 00:26:31.470
And we are going to
provide you with the tools

00:26:31.470 --> 00:26:36.430
and capabilities, so it's
very easy to use and deploy.

00:26:40.100 --> 00:26:45.270
We'll also be providing you with
the Edge TPU development kit.

00:26:45.270 --> 00:26:47.446
It will be coming in shortly.

00:26:47.446 --> 00:26:49.070
Essentially, it would
have an Edge TPU.

00:26:49.070 --> 00:26:52.010
It would have an
associated CPU, as well.

00:26:52.010 --> 00:26:53.340
It would have a secure element.

00:26:53.340 --> 00:26:55.370
We'll talk about it shortly.

00:26:55.370 --> 00:26:58.190
It'll all be
integrated on a system.

00:26:58.190 --> 00:27:01.070
It's called SOM, a
System On Chip module,

00:27:01.070 --> 00:27:07.380
which you could use to create
applications and develop

00:27:07.380 --> 00:27:11.120
these application before you
go into the full production.

00:27:11.120 --> 00:27:16.090
And this SOM would actually sit
on, for example, Raspberry Pi.

00:27:16.090 --> 00:27:18.740
And so that way, you could
reuse the development boards

00:27:18.740 --> 00:27:20.940
you already have in an
environment you have

00:27:20.940 --> 00:27:25.680
and just put in this new
SOM, which is already secure.

00:27:25.680 --> 00:27:28.400
It connects seamlessly
to the cloud.

00:27:28.400 --> 00:27:30.950
It has security built-in.

00:27:30.950 --> 00:27:33.620
And that will allow you
to improve and increase

00:27:33.620 --> 00:27:37.330
your go-to-market.

00:27:37.330 --> 00:27:41.690
We will also have a
USB connected Edge TPU.

00:27:41.690 --> 00:27:47.360
These are the AIY boards which
you can connect to your laptop

00:27:47.360 --> 00:27:48.490
or development environment.

00:27:48.490 --> 00:27:52.230
And then you can do machine
learning on these, as well.

00:27:52.230 --> 00:27:52.730
OK.

00:27:52.730 --> 00:27:59.860
So let's talk about Cloud
IoT core and security.

00:27:59.860 --> 00:28:03.190
So Cloud IoT core I mentioned
is the entrance point

00:28:03.190 --> 00:28:05.930
for all the IoT data.

00:28:05.930 --> 00:28:07.714
But what really is it?

00:28:07.714 --> 00:28:09.380
So basically, there
are two parts to it.

00:28:09.380 --> 00:28:10.742
And one is the protocol bridge.

00:28:10.742 --> 00:28:11.950
That's the connectivity part.

00:28:11.950 --> 00:28:14.620
And that's essentially your
connectivity through MQTT

00:28:14.620 --> 00:28:16.840
and HTTPS protocols.

00:28:16.840 --> 00:28:20.170
It does automatic load
balances, takes the data up

00:28:20.170 --> 00:28:23.170
from the devices and
puts it into the Pub/Sub.

00:28:23.170 --> 00:28:29.150
The second aspect to it is
this entire device shadow, this

00:28:29.150 --> 00:28:31.370
[INAUDIBLE],, like
what those devices are

00:28:31.370 --> 00:28:33.260
and how they are managed.

00:28:33.260 --> 00:28:34.870
So essentially,
all these devices

00:28:34.870 --> 00:28:38.350
are added into projects
and registries.

00:28:38.350 --> 00:28:40.900
And for each device,
there is a device ID

00:28:40.900 --> 00:28:44.960
and additional attributes which
are required for a connection--

00:28:44.960 --> 00:28:47.890
so for example, with a
public key or a certificate.

00:28:47.890 --> 00:28:51.040
And then there will be
additional metadata and state

00:28:51.040 --> 00:28:56.230
information about the device,
its health, and business

00:28:56.230 --> 00:29:00.600
attributes of these devices, but
also the telemetry data which

00:29:00.600 --> 00:29:01.850
is coming through the devices.

00:29:01.850 --> 00:29:04.150
For example, temperature,
humidity, motion,

00:29:04.150 --> 00:29:08.020
and all these different sensors
which are on the devices

00:29:08.020 --> 00:29:10.720
would be on that.

00:29:10.720 --> 00:29:14.310
So one of the very important
things about these devices

00:29:14.310 --> 00:29:16.690
and IoT in general is security.

00:29:16.690 --> 00:29:19.900
And we formally, Google
has a very strong opinion

00:29:19.900 --> 00:29:21.240
on security.

00:29:21.240 --> 00:29:23.240
And we have been pushing
the boundaries on that.

00:29:23.240 --> 00:29:30.565
So the basic idea is that we
use essentially asymmetric keys.

00:29:30.565 --> 00:29:34.090
So private key is
built into the device.

00:29:34.090 --> 00:29:37.510
And the public key of
that is in the cloud.

00:29:37.510 --> 00:29:42.510
And the way it works is that
the device, it's over TLS,

00:29:42.510 --> 00:29:45.130
but the way it works
is through JWT tokens.

00:29:45.130 --> 00:29:47.070
So the device will
create a JWT token

00:29:47.070 --> 00:29:49.120
and sign it using
the private key.

00:29:49.120 --> 00:29:52.990
And this private key is
then sent over to the Cloud.

00:29:52.990 --> 00:29:56.300
And that signature-- the
private key is not sent,

00:29:56.300 --> 00:29:58.840
the signed JWT is
actually signed.

00:29:58.840 --> 00:30:02.530
And the JWT signature is
verified by the cloud.

00:30:02.530 --> 00:30:07.057
And that's how the
connection is established.

00:30:07.057 --> 00:30:08.390
So there are problems with that.

00:30:08.390 --> 00:30:10.960
One is that you have to make
sure that private key is

00:30:10.960 --> 00:30:12.280
safe and secure.

00:30:12.280 --> 00:30:14.682
And what you would
end up doing is,

00:30:14.682 --> 00:30:16.890
and that's what we recommend,
is using secure element

00:30:16.890 --> 00:30:20.500
from microchip or NXB, which
has a private key built into it

00:30:20.500 --> 00:30:22.480
at the manufacturing time.

00:30:22.480 --> 00:30:25.210
And then you use
this private key.

00:30:25.210 --> 00:30:27.930
Obviously, you cannot
use a private directly,

00:30:27.930 --> 00:30:30.010
so you would end up having
the public key equal.

00:30:30.010 --> 00:30:32.410
And you get that from
the manufacturer.

00:30:32.410 --> 00:30:34.540
And you would put that
public key onto the cloud.

00:30:34.540 --> 00:30:38.730
And when the device boots
up, it does the same thing.

00:30:38.730 --> 00:30:40.910
It does the JWT
creation, signs it,

00:30:40.910 --> 00:30:42.720
and associates with the cloud.

00:30:42.720 --> 00:30:47.500
Now, this is a very
cumbersome process

00:30:47.500 --> 00:30:52.090
where you have to write a script
and have these public keys sent

00:30:52.090 --> 00:30:54.850
to the Device Manager.

00:30:54.850 --> 00:30:59.500
So what we have come up
with and tried to solve for

00:30:59.500 --> 00:31:00.970
is with the Cloud
IoT Provisioning.

00:31:00.970 --> 00:31:06.250
This is a new service which
basically allows you to manage

00:31:06.250 --> 00:31:07.540
this entire process easily.

00:31:07.540 --> 00:31:10.330
So to recap, really, there
are two problems, right?

00:31:10.330 --> 00:31:12.230
So one is this key manager.

00:31:12.230 --> 00:31:13.870
You have to secure these keys.

00:31:13.870 --> 00:31:16.360
You have to transfer these
public keys from the SE vendor

00:31:16.360 --> 00:31:18.430
to the OEM.

00:31:18.430 --> 00:31:21.820
And then there is the
device management part of it

00:31:21.820 --> 00:31:24.100
is that, OK, now you
have to make sure

00:31:24.100 --> 00:31:28.600
that these public keys and
device IDs are preregistered

00:31:28.600 --> 00:31:29.560
onto the cloud.

00:31:29.560 --> 00:31:34.270
And the same device ID and
the target information,

00:31:34.270 --> 00:31:36.310
which project ID and
registry ID needs

00:31:36.310 --> 00:31:38.585
to be actually on the firmware.

00:31:38.585 --> 00:31:41.530
So that means that you have
to pay a lot of attention.

00:31:41.530 --> 00:31:43.804
It becomes a complex process.

00:31:43.804 --> 00:31:45.220
So what we're
trying to do here is

00:31:45.220 --> 00:31:49.300
we're working with silicon
vendors, select silicon vendors

00:31:49.300 --> 00:31:53.350
to get these public keys
from them which are stored.

00:31:53.350 --> 00:31:58.390
You will simply use Reel ID or
UDID, or additional information

00:31:58.390 --> 00:32:00.220
for security sake.

00:32:00.220 --> 00:32:02.480
To get those and
claim those devices,

00:32:02.480 --> 00:32:04.940
you will simply easily
create your targets,

00:32:04.940 --> 00:32:08.770
which is simply your
registries where you

00:32:08.770 --> 00:32:10.060
want to put those devices in.

00:32:10.060 --> 00:32:13.120
And when the device
wakes up, it simply

00:32:13.120 --> 00:32:16.520
asks for where it needs
to go, and it goes there.

00:32:16.520 --> 00:32:19.150
So this is exactly what's
happening in this slide.

00:32:19.150 --> 00:32:23.320
As I said, it's the same exact
slide as the previous one,

00:32:23.320 --> 00:32:26.440
but the part here is that there
is a cloud IoT Provisioning

00:32:26.440 --> 00:32:30.970
service which gets the public
keys from the silicon vendors.

00:32:30.970 --> 00:32:33.220
And it allows you to
claim them, and it

00:32:33.220 --> 00:32:36.070
allows you to
identify these devices

00:32:36.070 --> 00:32:39.370
and provide the
related configuration

00:32:39.370 --> 00:32:42.490
and also provide information
where these devices need to go.

00:32:42.490 --> 00:32:45.790
It adds the target on to the
Device Manager in the cloud.

00:32:45.790 --> 00:32:47.486
And so when the
device wakes up it--

00:32:47.486 --> 00:32:49.110
and all these devices
are very generic.

00:32:49.110 --> 00:32:50.680
You don't have to worry
about these devices.

00:32:50.680 --> 00:32:51.929
They are very generic devices.

00:32:51.929 --> 00:32:56.110
They go and basically
say, OK, I'm

00:32:56.110 --> 00:32:58.600
X, Y, Z. Where am
I supposed to go?

00:32:58.600 --> 00:33:02.050
It gets the new config,
which tells it where to go.

00:33:02.050 --> 00:33:04.220
And this config could be
as elaborate as you want.

00:33:04.220 --> 00:33:05.345
It could be a new firmware.

00:33:05.345 --> 00:33:09.640
It could be any things you
want this device to do.

00:33:09.640 --> 00:33:12.280
And then it comes
back as a new device

00:33:12.280 --> 00:33:13.630
and connects to Google Cloud.

00:33:13.630 --> 00:33:17.170
And that's how it gets
authenticated and starts

00:33:17.170 --> 00:33:19.570
serving.

00:33:19.570 --> 00:33:25.230
So let me do a quick
demo of this service.

00:33:25.230 --> 00:33:27.700
And this is available
for early access,

00:33:27.700 --> 00:33:30.320
but let me give you
a quick snapshot.

00:33:30.320 --> 00:33:32.990
For example, when I
log in as a customer,

00:33:32.990 --> 00:33:34.810
I'm going to be looking
at a blank screen,

00:33:34.810 --> 00:33:36.780
obviously, because there
are no elements I have made.

00:33:36.780 --> 00:33:37.990
So I'm going to go in here.

00:33:37.990 --> 00:33:39.835
In this use case
I'm using Reel IDs.

00:33:39.835 --> 00:33:41.490
So I'm going to have a Reel ID.

00:33:41.490 --> 00:33:45.370
Reels are essentially all the
secure elements are chips.

00:33:45.370 --> 00:33:47.290
They have wound up on a tape.

00:33:47.290 --> 00:33:49.040
And it's wound up in a reel.

00:33:49.040 --> 00:33:49.900
And it's a package.

00:33:49.900 --> 00:33:51.940
It's essentially the package ID.

00:33:51.940 --> 00:33:55.810
So I'm going to just
put a package ID here,

00:33:55.810 --> 00:33:58.580
and I'm going to just claim it.

00:33:58.580 --> 00:34:01.690
So it's going to basically
go through these reels.

00:34:01.690 --> 00:34:07.380
And these reels
now have shown up.

00:34:07.380 --> 00:34:09.130
And these reels
have been claimed,

00:34:09.130 --> 00:34:10.560
but they have not been targeted.

00:34:10.560 --> 00:34:13.800
So now, I'm going to be
targeting into a registry.

00:34:13.800 --> 00:34:16.679
So I have a registry open here.

00:34:16.679 --> 00:34:19.158
And you can see
this is all empty

00:34:19.158 --> 00:34:20.699
right now, so I'm
going to be putting

00:34:20.699 --> 00:34:23.190
these devices in this registry.

00:34:23.190 --> 00:34:25.220
And there is a registry--

00:34:25.220 --> 00:34:29.040
-1 is a registry
in this project ID.

00:34:29.040 --> 00:34:31.510
And that's exactly what
I'm going to be doing here.

00:34:31.510 --> 00:34:38.250
So I'm going to target it to
this particular test target

00:34:38.250 --> 00:34:39.360
here.

00:34:39.360 --> 00:34:40.651
And I'm going to assign it.

00:34:40.651 --> 00:34:42.150
So essentially what
it's going to do

00:34:42.150 --> 00:34:51.236
is move these devices from
this place to the registry.

00:34:51.236 --> 00:34:52.819
So basically what
it's doing right now

00:34:52.819 --> 00:34:55.620
is it's going through
these in the loop,

00:34:55.620 --> 00:34:59.042
checking for these devices.

00:34:59.042 --> 00:35:00.750
And these devices are
now actually moving

00:35:00.750 --> 00:35:03.070
into the registry.

00:35:03.070 --> 00:35:07.610
So when I'm going to
try to refresh here,

00:35:07.610 --> 00:35:12.550
I should be able to see shortly.

00:35:12.550 --> 00:35:15.210
The devices should show up.

00:35:15.210 --> 00:35:20.550
But if you want to, this is
available as an early access.

00:35:20.550 --> 00:35:23.350
And you can sign up
for this early access

00:35:23.350 --> 00:35:28.000
by clicking onto this link here.

00:35:28.000 --> 00:35:30.310
And then we will
get back to you.

00:35:30.310 --> 00:35:32.270
We want to understand
your use cases, as well.

00:35:32.270 --> 00:35:35.730
And then we'll provide
you an early access.

00:35:35.730 --> 00:35:39.750
So hopefully, it would
have come up by now.

00:35:39.750 --> 00:35:40.250
OK.

00:35:40.250 --> 00:35:43.820
So now, these
devices have come up.

00:35:43.820 --> 00:35:45.480
And you can see
all these devices

00:35:45.480 --> 00:35:46.820
which were empty before.

00:35:46.820 --> 00:35:51.110
And then it's very easy for
us to move from these devices.

00:35:51.110 --> 00:35:52.940
And here, these devices
have been empty.

00:35:52.940 --> 00:35:55.140
So next time you have a
next batch of devices,

00:35:55.140 --> 00:35:56.907
you come in and do the
exact same process.

00:35:56.907 --> 00:35:59.240
So you don't have to worry
about where the devices went.

00:35:59.240 --> 00:36:00.710
These all devices
are very generic.

00:36:00.710 --> 00:36:02.710
When they come up, they
get their configuration,

00:36:02.710 --> 00:36:05.510
and then they connect.

00:36:05.510 --> 00:36:11.040
With that, we're going
to end the session here.

00:36:11.040 --> 00:36:12.680
This is going to
be our last slide.

00:36:12.680 --> 00:36:14.990
And then stay tuned
for Q&amp;A. And we'll be

00:36:14.990 --> 00:36:17.270
right back in a few minutes--

00:36:17.270 --> 00:36:18.660
maybe less than a minute.

00:36:18.660 --> 00:36:21.410
And then we'll take up the
questions which you may have.

00:36:21.410 --> 00:36:24.320
So feel free to give us any
questions, any feedback.

00:36:24.320 --> 00:36:26.040
And then we'll be
right back with you.

00:36:26.040 --> 00:36:26.540
Thank you.

00:37:21.100 --> 00:37:21.700
OK.

00:37:21.700 --> 00:37:22.730
Welcome back.

00:37:22.730 --> 00:37:25.020
So it looks like we have
three questions here.

00:37:25.020 --> 00:37:27.060
So let's get going
with the questions.

00:37:27.060 --> 00:37:30.720
So first question is, in terms
of Cloud IoT Provisioning,

00:37:30.720 --> 00:37:33.240
I don't have secure
element right now,

00:37:33.240 --> 00:37:35.610
but plan to use it
in future products.

00:37:35.610 --> 00:37:38.850
Can I still use this service?

00:37:38.850 --> 00:37:39.860
Yes.

00:37:39.860 --> 00:37:43.374
So clearly, we are trying to
solve for the secure element

00:37:43.374 --> 00:37:45.540
use cases, but that doesn't
mean that's the only use

00:37:45.540 --> 00:37:47.350
cases we wanted to solve for.

00:37:47.350 --> 00:37:50.370
So as we go through
this process,

00:37:50.370 --> 00:37:53.520
we will be solving for the
non-secure element use cases,

00:37:53.520 --> 00:37:57.600
because there are
ways for you to create

00:37:57.600 --> 00:37:59.860
public, private key pairs today.

00:37:59.860 --> 00:38:02.250
And we want to be able
to solve for the part

00:38:02.250 --> 00:38:06.330
where you're confident
about the private key,

00:38:06.330 --> 00:38:10.860
but the public key still needs
to be connected, and put that

00:38:10.860 --> 00:38:13.357
into the cloud repository.

00:38:13.357 --> 00:38:14.940
And we want to make
that process easy.

00:38:14.940 --> 00:38:15.500
So yes.

00:38:15.500 --> 00:38:17.415
That's the short answer.

00:38:17.415 --> 00:38:18.090
So let's see.

00:38:18.090 --> 00:38:20.390
The next question
is, do I have to use

00:38:20.390 --> 00:38:24.890
Edge TPU to take advantage
of Google's Edge processing?

00:38:27.280 --> 00:38:27.780
OK.

00:38:27.780 --> 00:38:28.840
So yeah, no.

00:38:28.840 --> 00:38:30.640
I mean, clearly,
Edge TPU gives you

00:38:30.640 --> 00:38:33.700
that advantage for
the use cases where

00:38:33.700 --> 00:38:35.020
you need heavy processing.

00:38:35.020 --> 00:38:36.700
But there are a lot
of Edge use cases

00:38:36.700 --> 00:38:40.840
where maybe general purpose
CPUs is perfectly fine.

00:38:40.840 --> 00:38:43.330
Because even on the
ML and AI, there's

00:38:43.330 --> 00:38:46.330
a huge range of
computational requirements.

00:38:46.330 --> 00:38:47.440
So no.

00:38:47.440 --> 00:38:52.660
We wouldn't support GPUs or
support general purpose CPUs.

00:38:52.660 --> 00:38:56.860
And whatever your model
is which you have created,

00:38:56.860 --> 00:38:59.590
it can be used
through our products

00:38:59.590 --> 00:39:07.400
to send back those models from
Cloud IoT to Cloud IoT Edge.

00:39:07.400 --> 00:39:09.460
And then you can
use these models

00:39:09.460 --> 00:39:11.630
for inference in the Edge.

00:39:11.630 --> 00:39:13.690
And then, if you're
going to use TPU,

00:39:13.690 --> 00:39:15.230
then we will support that too.

00:39:15.230 --> 00:39:21.340
So Edge TPU's not the only
thing we're supporting here.

00:39:21.340 --> 00:39:23.575
Next question we have
is, can we configure

00:39:23.575 --> 00:39:27.010
Cloud IoT Edge,
plus Android Things,

00:39:27.010 --> 00:39:31.831
plus Edge TPU on the currently
available Raspberry Pi?

00:39:31.831 --> 00:39:32.330
Yes.

00:39:32.330 --> 00:39:35.500
So as I mentioned,
it is a SOM, which

00:39:35.500 --> 00:39:39.340
is essentially going to have
all these things in one SOM.

00:39:39.340 --> 00:39:45.490
And yes, you could use the
Raspberry Pi with that SOM.

00:39:45.490 --> 00:39:47.290
But when that SOM is
going to be available?

00:39:47.290 --> 00:39:50.860
Sometime in the upcoming
months it will be available.

00:39:50.860 --> 00:39:53.890
So you want to sign up
for that early access.

00:39:53.890 --> 00:39:54.860
So let us know.

00:39:54.860 --> 00:39:56.440
We want to work with you.

00:39:56.440 --> 00:40:00.261
And so make sure that you are
one of the first customers.

00:40:00.261 --> 00:40:00.760
But yes.

00:40:00.760 --> 00:40:02.680
You could use any general
purpose Raspberry Pi.

00:40:02.680 --> 00:40:04.263
And that's the whole
idea of that SOM.

00:40:04.263 --> 00:40:06.590
It just goes on top of it.

00:40:06.590 --> 00:40:08.920
And you can start off
with your projects

00:40:08.920 --> 00:40:10.252
right now without the Edge TPU.

00:40:10.252 --> 00:40:11.710
And then when that's
available, you

00:40:11.710 --> 00:40:17.750
can make that Edge TPU capable.

00:40:17.750 --> 00:40:18.250
OK.

00:40:18.250 --> 00:40:21.100
Well, thanks for
all these questions.

00:40:21.100 --> 00:40:22.730
And thanks for your time.

00:40:22.730 --> 00:40:26.360
Stay tuned for the next session.

